\subsection{Limitations}
Our evaluation focuses on scene- and appearance-induced static biases; demographic and broader real-world fairness dimensions remain unexplored. Treating static information as uniformly unhelpful may be too strict for actions where static cues are legitimately discriminative; future work could retain beneficial spatial cues while suppressing spurious ones. IID accuracy on HMDB51 and UCF101 is slightly lower than the baseline. Static frames could also be selected by a learned indicator such as action saliency. Finally, the improved protocol relies on automatic segmentation followed by manual checking.

\section{Conclusion}
\approachname{} debiases action recognition by breaking the correlation between labels and static cues inside a single-encoder, label-free adversarial framework, and the corrected UCF101 SCUBA/SCUFO protocol removes background leakage for fairer evaluation. It improves contrasted accuracy on HMDB51 by 12.9 points over prior methods and shows gains on the improved UCF101 protocol and on downstream tasks.

